    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `extract_invoice` in `app/invoices.py`, and it uses structured outputs as you asked. It sends one `client.beta.messages.create` call with `model=MODEL`, `max_tokens=1024`, `betas=[FILES_BETA]` and the invoice as a document block, like `summarize_invoice` does. It also passes `output_config={\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": INVOICE_SCHEMA}}`, so the API itself limits the reply text to the schema. The prompt doesn't ask for JSON, and there are no tools. The function joins the reply's text blocks and returns them decoded with `json.loads`.\n\n**One addition you didn't ask for:** if the reply ends for any reason other than `end_turn` (for example a refusal, or hitting the `max_tokens` limit), the function raises a `RuntimeError` instead of decoding. In those cases the reply isn't guaranteed to match the schema, and a half-finished row shouldn't go into the ledger.\n\n**Testing:**\n- `pytest -q`: both existing tests pass. None of them cover `extract_invoice`.\n- I ran a throwaway script with a fake HTTP layer, so no real API call was made. The request had the files beta header, `max_tokens` 1024, model `claude-opus-5-5`, the schema under `output_config.format`, no `tools`, and the file-id document block. The returned dict matched the expected row.\n- I haven't tried it against the live API with a real uploaded invoice.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** my permissions didn't allow me to delete that script, `_check.py`, from the project root. Please remove it."
